<div class="article-body">

<p>I spent $47 on organic salad greens last month. That's down from $63 the month before, because I'd started growing my own.</p>

<p>My first hydroponic setup cost $117 and paid for itself in 8 weeks. Now I have fresh lettuce, basil, and kale growing year round on a shelf in my kitchen. No backyard. No expensive equipment.</p>

<p>This is the guide I wish existed when I started. If you can follow a recipe, you can build a system that grows food faster than anything you'll buy at the store.</p>

<h2 id="why">1. Why Bother With Hydroponics?</h2>

<p>Here's a number that made me swear out loud: the average head of lettuce in a US grocery store travels <strong>2,000 miles</strong> from farm to shelf. It was picked 2 to 3 weeks before you eat it. By the time it hits your kitchen, it's lost 30 to 50% of its nutrients.</p>

<p>Hydroponic lettuce grown in your kitchen goes from seed to salad in 30 days and you eat it 10 minutes after harvesting.</p>

<p><strong>Let's talk money. That's what actually matters.</strong></p>

<ul>
  <li>One hydroponic lettuce plant produces $3 to $4 worth of greens per week for 8 to 12 weeks</li>
  <li>A 6 plant setup = $18 to $24/week in grocery savings</li>
  <li>Over a year: <strong>$900 to $1,200 saved on produce alone</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>And that's just lettuce. When you factor in basil, kale, mint, and cherry tomatoes, the savings go up fast. A single basil plant in hydroponics will produce more than you can use. I give mine away to neighbors now.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">💰 Quick Math</div>
  <p>One 10 gallon DWC system + LED light + nutrients = about $150 upfront. Compare that to $15/week on organic salad greens. Break even at week 10. Everything after that is profit.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="how">2. The 2 Systems That Actually Work</h2>

<p>There are about 47 ways to grow without soil. These two are the only ones a beginner should care about.</p>

<img src="/images/hydro-systems.svg" alt="Two beginner hydroponic systems compared side by side" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;" width="600" height="320" />

<h3>Kratky Method. The "Set It and Forget It" Champion</h3>
<p><strong>Difficulty: 1/10. Cost: $5 to $20. Best for: Lettuce, herbs, microgreens</strong></p>

<p>The Kratky method is named after a Hawaiian scientist who realized you don't need pumps or electricity. You fill a container with nutrient water, put a net cup with a seedling in the top, and walk away. The plant drinks the water. As the level drops, an air gap forms. The plant grows air roots. It's self regulating.</p>

<p>I grew my first hydroponic basil in a repurposed pickle jar using this method. Three months later it was 2 feet tall. No pumps. No electricity except a cheap grow light. No maintenance except refilling water every 2 to 3 weeks.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Best for people who want to try hydroponics for under $20</div>
  <p>Get a quart mason jar, a 2 inch net cup, some clay pebbles, and hydroponic nutrient solution. Total is about $15. Start with basil seeds. If you fail, you're out $15. If you succeed, you never buy basil again.</p>
</div>

<h3>Deep Water Culture (DWC). The Gold Standard for Home Growers</h3>
<p><strong>Difficulty: 3/10. Cost: $50 to $150. Best for: Lettuce, kale, chard, peppers</strong></p>

<p>DWC is what 80% of home hydroponic growers use. A container holds nutrient water. An aquarium air pump bubbles oxygen into the water through an air stone. The roots hang directly in the oxygenated nutrient solution. Growth rates are 30 to 50% faster than soil.</p>

<p>This is what I use for my main setup. The pump noise took about 3 days to get used to. Buy a whisper quiet pump. Trust me, spend the extra $10. Once it's running, you check pH once a week, change the water every 2 weeks, and harvest every single day.</p>

