<h2 id="mistakes">4. The #1 Mistake That Kills First Time Setups</h2>

<p>I made this mistake. Every single person I've taught has made this mistake. You will probably make it too.</p>

<p><strong>The mistake: overwatering. In a hydroponic system.</strong></p>

<p>Wait, how do you overwater in water? Here's how. When you first move a seedling into a hydro system, the roots need oxygen. If you submerge the whole root system in water without an air stone, the roots suffocate. The plant wilts. You panic and add more water. It dies faster.</p>

<p><strong>The fix is stupidly simple.</strong></p>

<p>For the first 3 to 5 days after transplanting, keep the water level just below the net cup. The roots should reach down into the water, not be fully submerged. This forces roots to grow downward seeking moisture. After 5 days, fill to the normal level.</p>

<p><strong>Second mistake: ignoring pH.</strong> Your plants could have perfect light and perfect temperature and still look like garbage because the pH is 8.0. Plants absorb nutrients only within a specific pH range (5.5 to 6.5 for hydroponics). Outside that range, nutrients exist in the water but the plant can't absorb them.</p>

<p>A $15 digital pH meter pays for itself in one grow cycle.</p>

